20
00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:18.240
But what I want to show you is how you can
build a trigger word detection system.

21
00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.512
The literature on trigger detection
algorithm is still evolving so there isn't

22
00:01:22.512 --> 00:01:26.690
wide consensus yet on what's the best
algorithm for trigger word detection.

23
00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:30.842
So I'm just going to show you one
example of an algorithm you can use.

24
00:01:30.842 --> 00:01:35.736
Now, you've seen RNNs like this and
what we really do is take an audio clip,

25
00:01:35.736 --> 00:01:38.263
maybe compute spectrogram features.

26
00:01:38.263 --> 00:01:42.940
And that generates features, x1,
x2, x3 audio features, x1, x2,

27
00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:45.010
x3 that you pass through an RNN.

28
00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:50.440
And so all that remains to be done
is to define the target labels.

29
00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:51.640
Y

30
00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:56.802
So if this point in the audio clip
is when someone just finished saying

31
00:01:56.802 --> 00:02:01.875
the trigger word, such as Alexa or
xiaodunihao or hey, Siri, or okay,

32
00:02:01.875 --> 00:02:07.571
Google, then in the training sets,
you can set the target labels to be 0 for

33
00:02:07.571 --> 00:02:13.563
everything before that point and right
after that to set the target label of 1.

34
00:02:13.563 --> 00:02:19.059
And then if a little bit later on,
the trigger word was said again and

35
00:02:19.059 --> 00:02:22.338
the trigger word was said at this point,

36
00:02:22.338 --> 00:02:27.561
then you can again set the target
label to be 1 right after that.

37
00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:34.550
Now, this type of labelling scheme for
an RNN could work.

38
00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:37.150
Actually, this will actually
work reasonably well.

39
00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:41.802
One slight disadvantage of this
is it creates a very imbalanced

40
00:02:41.802 --> 00:02:45.440
training set to have
a lot more 0s than 1s.

41
00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:49.756
So one other thing you could do,
this is a little bit of a hack, but

42
00:02:49.756 --> 00:02:54.624
could make the model a little bit easier
to train is instead of setting only

43
00:02:54.624 --> 00:02:59.410
a single time step output 1, you can
actually make it output a few 1s for

44
00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:04.840
several times or for a fixed period
of time before reverting back to 0.

45
00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:12.431
So and that slightly evens
out the ratio of 1s to 0s,

46
00:03:12.431 --> 00:03:17.167
but this is a little bit of a hack.

47
00:03:17.167 --> 00:03:22.120
But if this is when in the audio
clipper the trigger word is said,

48
00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:26.889
then right after that,
you can set the target label to 1, and

49
00:03:26.889 --> 00:03:29.916
if this is the trigger word said again,

50
00:03:29.916 --> 00:03:34.461
then right after that is when
you want the RNN to output 1.

51
